From 1cd80cc8c85c6a1a01aee6eb37c767497f5f6d48 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 21:56:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 74/74] hir_typeck: a recursive impl search in the
 current-trait method step assumes the method exists

`impl<'v, T> PartialEq<&[T]> for Value<'v> where Value<'v>: PartialEq<T>` calling
`t.eq(other)` on `t: &Vec<Value<'v>>` (simd-json 0.18.1 value/borrowed/cmp.rs:169)
failed with "No applicable methods for {&Vec<Value>}.eq".

`find_method` step 2 (the trait of the enclosing impl) found `eq`, then searched
for `Vec<Value>: PartialEq<_>`; alloc's `impl<T, U> PartialEq<[U]> for Vec<T> where
T: PartialEq<U>` made it check `Value: PartialEq<?U>`, whose candidates include the
impl being written, whose own where-clause is the same query again, so
`find_trait_impls_crate` threw `RecursionDetected`. The handler returned `false`,
which left `find_method` entirely, skipping the remaining steps for that autoref
level, and the lookup ended without a candidate.

The in-scope trait step already treats this recursion as "assume good" (upstream
d6ae2e55): the trait params are ivars, so the pending bound is re-checked once the
argument type pins them down. Do the same here. The call then resolves to
`<Vec<Value> as PartialEq<&[T]>>::eq`, with `Value: PartialEq<T>` satisfied by the
impl's where-clause, which is what rustc selects (an ambiguous obligation still
counts as an applicable candidate at that probe step).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/hir_typeck/helpers.cpp | 11 +++++++++--
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/hir_typeck/helpers.cpp b/src/hir_typeck/helpers.cpp
index c8f72302..4c8103a8 100644
--- a/src/hir_typeck/helpers.cpp
+++ b/src/hir_typeck/helpers.cpp
@@ -5155,8 +5155,15 @@ bool TraitResolution::find_method(const Span& sp,
                     {
                     }
                 } catch(const TraitResolution::RecursionDetected& ) {
-                    DEBUG("Recursion detected, deferring");
-                    return false;
+                    // The impl search looped back on itself (e.g. `impl<T> PartialEq<&[T]> for V where V: PartialEq<T>`
+                    // checking `V: PartialEq<_>` for a `Vec<V>` receiver). Like the in-scope trait search below,
+                    // assume the method exists: the trait params are ivars, so the bound is re-checked once the
+                    // arguments pin them down. Bailing out here skipped the remaining steps and errored with
+                    // "No applicable methods" on a call rustc accepts.
+                    DEBUG("Recursion detected, assuming good");
+                    possibilities.push_back(::std::make_pair( borrow_type, ::HIR::Path(self_ty->clone(), ::HIR::GenericPath( final_trait_path.m_path, mv$(trait_params) ), method_name, {}) ));
+                    DEBUG("++ " << possibilities.back());
+                    return true;
                 }
             }
         }
